<!--
  Libvirt domain XML template for one L3 test VM (doc 06 §4). Rendered by
  create-vm.sh, never hand-edited per lab — a generated artifact, the same
  posture doc 14 §2 holds Hoserva's own future domain XML to.

  Networking is deliberately usermode (QEMU's own SLIRP stack): under
  qemu:///session there is no privileged helper to attach a bridge or a
  libvirt-managed NAT network, and usermode networking needs no host-side
  network config, no polkit interaction and leaves nothing standing after
  the domain is undefined — exactly the posture CLAUDE.md requires (never
  touch host network/mount config). The two hostfwd rules are this lab's
  own SSH and hoservad TLS ports, bound to loopback only (127.0.0.1) so
  nothing here is ever reachable off the dev host.

  hostfwd is expressed through <qemu:commandline> (-netdev user,hostfwd=…)
  rather than libvirt's native <interface type='user'> device: libvirt's
  own declarative <portForward> element requires the 'passt' backend,
  which this host does not have installed (and installing it would be a
  package-manager install this project's agents never run) — confirmed by
  running this exact template against this host's real libvirt/QEMU
  (`unsupported configuration: The <portForward> element can only be used
  with the 'passt' backend`). qemu:commandline's own SLIRP hostfwd needs
  no extra package, matching the plain SLIRP QEMU already ships.

  machine='pc' (i440fx), not 'q35', and the explicit <video> device
  below: both found empirically on this host, by bisecting a hand-built
  qemu-system-x86_64 invocation one flag at a time (never guessed at)
  after the guest kept resetting under `make vm-up` — the serial log
  showed GRUB's "Booting `Debian GNU/Linux'" banner replayed hundreds of
  times over several minutes, with Linux never once printing a line.

  Root cause, isolated by bisection down to one variable at a time:
  libvirt always passes QEMU `-nodefaults` (so a domain never depends on
  QEMU's own default-device guesses); on this host, with `-nodefaults`
  and NO video device at all — this harness has no graphics, only the
  serial console, so there had never seemed to be a reason to add one —
  SeaBIOS resets the machine right as it hands off to the Linux kernel,
  every single time, with or without q35, regardless of which PCI slot
  the boot disk sits on. Reproduced outside libvirt entirely with a bare
  qemu-system-x86_64 invocation (ruling out libvirtd, its QMP heartbeat,
  and its seccomp sandbox), and confirmed with a true single-variable
  A/B on that same invocation: identical in every other respect,
  `-nodefaults` alone reproduces the reset, and adding back one explicit
  `-vga std` on top of `-nodefaults` boots the identical disk clean
  every time — the fix is exactly this <video> element, never omitted
  even though its framebuffer is never read. 'pc' over 'q35' is kept as
  a second, independent finding from earlier in this same bisection
  (q35's default PCIe root-port topology and its default ICH9 TCO
  watchdog each independently reproduced a similarly-shaped reset loop
  before <video> was identified as q35's own trigger too; 'pc' is the
  simpler machine type and was not revisited against the <video> fix,
  so it remains the one this template uses).

  Placeholders (__UPPER_CASE__) are substituted by create-vm.sh with
  values it built itself (paths under this lab's own .vm/<id>/, numeric
  ports, its own generated MAC) — never with user- or template-supplied
  text, so this substitution is plain text replacement, not a shell or
  XML injection surface.
-->
<domain type='kvm' xmlns:qemu='http://libvirt.org/schemas/domain/qemu/1.0'>
  <name>__DOMAIN_NAME__</name>
  <memory unit='MiB'>__MEMORY_MIB__</memory>
  <currentMemory unit='MiB'>__MEMORY_MIB__</currentMemory>
  <vcpu>__VCPU_COUNT__</vcpu>
  <os>
    <type arch='x86_64' machine='pc'>hvm</type>
    <boot dev='hd'/>
  </os>
  <features>
    <acpi/>
    <apic/>
  </features>
  <cpu mode='host-passthrough' check='none'/>
  <clock offset='utc'/>
  <on_poweroff>destroy</on_poweroff>
  <on_reboot>restart</on_reboot>
  <on_crash>destroy</on_crash>
  <devices>
    <emulator>/usr/bin/qemu-system-x86_64</emulator>
    <disk type='file' device='disk'>
      <driver name='qemu' type='qcow2'/>
      <source file='__OS_DISK_PATH__'/>
      <target dev='vda' bus='virtio'/>
      <address type='pci' domain='0x0000' bus='0x00' slot='0x09' function='0x0'/>
    </disk>
__ARRAY_DISKS__
    <disk type='file' device='cdrom'>
      <driver name='qemu' type='raw'/>
      <source file='__SEED_ISO_PATH__'/>
      <target dev='hda' bus='ide'/>
      <readonly/>
    </disk>
    <serial type='pty'>
      <target type='isa-serial' port='0'/>
      <log file='__SERIAL_LOG_PATH__' append='off'/>
    </serial>
    <!-- See this file's header comment: required for a clean boot on
         this host under `-nodefaults`, even though nothing ever reads
         its framebuffer — there is no <graphics> device, only the
         serial console above. -->
    <video>
      <model type='vga' vram='16384' heads='1' primary='yes'/>
    </video>
  </devices>
  <qemu:commandline>
    <qemu:arg value='-netdev'/>
    <qemu:arg value='user,id=hoservanet0,hostfwd=tcp:127.0.0.1:__SSH_PORT__-:22,hostfwd=tcp:127.0.0.1:__HTTPS_PORT__-:8008'/>
    <qemu:arg value='-device'/>
    <qemu:arg value='virtio-net-pci,netdev=hoservanet0,mac=__MAC_ADDRESS__,bus=pci.0,addr=0x14'/>
  </qemu:commandline>
</domain>
